1
00:00:02,943 --> 00:00:14,431
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of Sotapanapatti, it's stated in suttas that one should understand the consciousness.

2
00:00:15,492 --> 00:00:17,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness is to be understood.

3
00:00:19,154 --> 00:00:21,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, not in terms of Sotapanapatti.

4
00:00:21,656 --> 00:00:22,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

5
00:00:22,697 --> 00:00:25,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Complete understanding of consciousness is arahantship.

6
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:30,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

7
00:00:33,652 --> 00:00:44,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have completely understood Consciousness, means you have completely removed the slightest traces of conceit, conceivings, craving and so on.

8
00:00:44,854 --> 00:00:46,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means you're an Arhant.

9
00:00:48,955 --> 00:01:00,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... Sotāpanna should at least understand, like... One needs to understand what principle of freedom from suffering is.

10
00:01:02,525 --> 00:01:04,608
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what needs to be understood by Sotāpanna.

11
00:01:04,628 --> 00:01:05,829
[SPEAKER_00]: The Four Noble Truths, basically.

12
00:01:08,813 --> 00:01:16,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you were to follow the understanding of the Four Noble Truths, it would carry on into you, uprooting any conceivings.

13
00:01:19,805 --> 00:01:46,178
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, even when you arrive at things you read in the suttas, like, eh... consciousness to be understood, non-indicative, luminous, all and whatever those translations are, from different translators, that sounds abstract now, because it kind of is, but even understanding of such abstract things can only happen rightly on the basis of practical application.

14
00:01:48,099 --> 00:01:48,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, when you think,

15
00:01:49,275 --> 00:01:59,785
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of the consciousness needs to be understood, you don't... you don't want to allow yourself to keep thinking it in the abstract domain, but you want to say, okay, so, am I conscious now?

16
00:02:01,507 --> 00:02:07,812
[SPEAKER_00]: You ask yourself that, because it's that consciousness, that you're conscious now, that's the consciousness that needs to be understood.

17
00:02:07,832 --> 00:02:21,804
[SPEAKER_00]: But see where if you approach it, how consciousness is this non-indicative awareness, luminous presence, whatever you translate it with, it's already an abstract category that you will be thinking about at the expense of your present enduring conscious experience as a whole.

18
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:27,509
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you'll never arrive at that with consciousnesses, because that's just another way of misconceiving it.

19
00:02:29,310 --> 00:02:33,492
[SPEAKER_00]: You start with the idea that's already affected with the wrong notion of what consciousness is.

20
00:02:34,713 --> 00:02:39,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to start like that, but you want to ask yourself now, am I conscious right now?

21
00:02:39,836 --> 00:02:41,357
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if I ask you now, are you conscious?

22
00:02:41,577 --> 00:02:41,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

23
00:02:42,717 --> 00:02:49,501
[SPEAKER_00]: When you say I am, when you say that there is consciousness there, what is it that you refer to?

24
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:52,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Me experiencing, me...

25
00:02:54,043 --> 00:02:56,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is there any other way you can know consciousness?

26
00:03:03,586 --> 00:03:09,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly, because if you can think of another way, it would be you experiencing your thinking, that you're conscious of.

27
00:03:10,849 --> 00:03:13,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, consciousness and experience are inseparable.

28
00:03:15,691 --> 00:03:18,933
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like there's consciousness and then there's the experience for the consciousness.

29
00:03:19,893 --> 00:03:28,277
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only discern consciousness as the presence of the experience, of any kind.

30
00:03:29,301 --> 00:03:31,002
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's always the five aggregates.

31
00:03:31,022 --> 00:03:34,665
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not four and one, or three and two, and two and a half, and two and a half.

32
00:03:35,666 --> 00:03:36,447
[SPEAKER_00]: It's five aggregates.

33
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:37,407
[SPEAKER_00]: The heap.

34
00:03:38,728 --> 00:03:39,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the conscious heap.

35
00:03:43,792 --> 00:03:49,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your experience right here, right now, you said, that's pretty much you're conscious of.

36
00:03:51,198 --> 00:03:51,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you...

37
00:03:54,130 --> 00:03:55,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what are the characteristics?

38
00:03:55,951 --> 00:03:59,392
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the characteristics you would... you might want to describe it?

39
00:03:59,693 --> 00:04:06,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be the characteristics of the consciousness, within that experience as a whole, that you are conscious of now?

40
00:04:21,425 --> 00:04:21,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness.

41
00:04:21,905 --> 00:04:22,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Describing the consciousness.

42
00:04:22,886 --> 00:04:24,027
[SPEAKER_00]: You said, well, it's the experience.

43
00:04:24,087 --> 00:04:25,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, what else can you say about it?

44
00:04:30,711 --> 00:04:33,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't think in terms of the stuff you read about consciousness.

45
00:04:33,894 --> 00:04:34,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

46
00:04:34,894 --> 00:04:36,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Think in terms of right now.

47
00:04:37,056 --> 00:04:38,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, I never heard a word consciousness.

48
00:04:39,438 --> 00:04:42,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And I come to you and I ask you, can you tell me what what do you mean by consciousness?

49
00:04:45,143 --> 00:04:47,324
[SPEAKER_00]: That I can relate in my experience right here, right now.

50
00:04:49,524 --> 00:05:14,452
[SPEAKER_01]: The ability to think, like, like, for me, it's, like, that's one of the reasons I actually asked about it, because it's one of the hardest that I found to be pinpointing without

51
00:05:17,226 --> 00:05:22,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to put it out there, sort of like a external object.

52
00:05:23,108 --> 00:05:24,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you pinpoint then?

53
00:05:26,909 --> 00:05:27,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there will be...

54
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:33,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't pinpoint, or rather you want to pinpoint, but you know you can't put it as an external object.

55
00:05:33,272 --> 00:05:39,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would you be pinpointing then, unless it's an external object?

56
00:05:42,335 --> 00:05:45,996
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's so hard for you to understand it, because your idea of it contradicts itself.

57
00:05:48,418 --> 00:05:48,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're right.

58
00:05:48,898 --> 00:05:51,879
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't pinpoint consciousness, because there will be something you're conscious of.

59
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:56,901
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you then describe the consciousness of something?

60
00:06:07,424 --> 00:06:10,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can only describe it on account of that thing that you're conscious of.

61
00:06:11,885 --> 00:06:15,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can only describe consciousness negatively.

62
00:06:15,987 --> 00:06:16,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

63
00:06:16,227 --> 00:06:16,947
[SPEAKER_00]: What it is not.

64
00:06:21,227 --> 00:06:25,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is defined by that which you are conscious of.

65
00:06:25,090 --> 00:06:26,871
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said there are six types of consciousness.

66
00:06:27,331 --> 00:06:31,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness of the eyes, nose, mouth, ear, body and mind.

67
00:06:35,937 --> 00:06:38,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sight defines the eye consciousness.

68
00:06:38,899 --> 00:06:41,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of the sight means there is consciousness there.

69
00:06:44,950 --> 00:06:45,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

70
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:46,250
[SPEAKER_00]: To see it.

71
00:06:46,670 --> 00:06:51,211
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is when people think consciousness, they think this kind of duality, like the world and me.

72
00:06:51,651 --> 00:06:52,272
[SPEAKER_00]: I am conscious.

73
00:06:52,312 --> 00:06:53,212
[SPEAKER_00]: My mind is conscious.

74
00:06:53,232 --> 00:06:53,952
[SPEAKER_00]: The world is there.

75
00:06:53,972 --> 00:06:54,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It's objective.

76
00:06:54,652 --> 00:06:56,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Subjective is where my consciousness is.

77
00:06:56,852 --> 00:06:59,753
[SPEAKER_00]: But all of that is already on the base of experience as a whole that you have.

78
00:07:00,533 --> 00:07:08,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Any division that you might have, me and the world, subject-object, is the result of the first things arising as a whole, within which you can focus on different aspects.

79
00:07:10,555 --> 00:07:15,439
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your consciousness is not more in you than these paññan roots are.

80
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:18,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

81
00:07:18,942 --> 00:07:30,773
[SPEAKER_00]: If consciousness is only define... definable by the sense... sense domains, means your consciousness is out there with the senses, not in your head, in your brain or in your mind.

82
00:07:31,433 --> 00:07:35,297
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the sixth consciousness class is present, so to speak.

83
00:07:36,267 --> 00:07:38,108
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not like one and then they take the place.

84
00:07:38,188 --> 00:07:43,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You have your eyes at the same time when there is your nose, your mouth, your body, your intellect.

85
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:46,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these six domains of consciousness are always there.

86
00:07:47,951 --> 00:07:50,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you can't attend to all of them at the same time.

87
00:07:51,407 --> 00:07:53,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Your attention will be drawn to one domain more than the other.

88
00:07:54,350 --> 00:07:54,691
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

89
00:07:55,372 --> 00:07:57,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Now we're talking, we're thinking, you're looking at me.

90
00:07:57,754 --> 00:08:01,118
[SPEAKER_00]: But at the back of your mind, there is the consciousness of sound still enduring.

91
00:08:01,178 --> 00:08:01,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

92
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:06,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Because halfway through our conversation, your attention can be drawn to sounds.

93
00:08:07,886 --> 00:08:09,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Or to the ant biting your foot.

94
00:08:10,813 --> 00:08:15,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Means the body of the six layers of consciousness, so to speak.

95
00:08:16,114 --> 00:08:17,614
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the six, it's always there.

96
00:08:17,955 --> 00:08:18,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it disappear?

97
00:08:18,975 --> 00:08:19,695
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't disappear.

98
00:08:19,875 --> 00:08:21,435
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only attend it or not attend it.

99
00:08:22,215 --> 00:08:25,296
[SPEAKER_00]: But, either way, you can only do that because it's already there.

100
00:08:25,996 --> 00:08:26,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

101
00:08:26,756 --> 00:08:32,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Practically speaking, now, your consciousness is out there with that body, with those senses, not in your head.

102
00:08:33,338 --> 00:08:38,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Your attention, it's really, if you want to say something's in my head, it's like what you choose to attend.

103
00:08:38,592 --> 00:08:41,733
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of given state of things.

104
00:08:43,073 --> 00:08:45,734
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that is not really in your head, because you can attend to your head.

105
00:08:47,354 --> 00:08:48,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, where's the consciousness then?

106
00:08:49,395 --> 00:08:50,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Your attention.

107
00:08:51,455 --> 00:08:56,576
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is to recognize that this gratuitous notion that consciousness is in me, is completely mistaken.

108
00:08:56,596 --> 00:08:59,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can start with a gratuitous notion, consciousness is in me.

109
00:08:59,897 --> 00:09:00,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, how do I know that?

110
00:09:01,278 --> 00:09:07,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because I'm experiencing the idea of inside of me consciousness being there, and I'm conscious of that whole thing as a whole.

111
00:09:08,607 --> 00:09:10,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're like, oh, so that cannot be my consciousness.

112
00:09:13,948 --> 00:09:21,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So consciousness, literally, the most accurately you can describe is its presence of things.

113
00:09:21,829 --> 00:09:23,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means there always has to be something present.

114
00:09:26,590 --> 00:09:31,591
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that it's present means consciousness is consciousness and things means presence of things.

115
00:09:32,251 --> 00:09:35,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Not I am conscious or I make the effort to be conscious.

116
00:09:35,512 --> 00:09:35,672
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

117
00:09:36,409 --> 00:09:44,951
[SPEAKER_00]: I can make the effort to attend this and not attend that, on account of it all being already within those sense domains that are conscious on their own.

118
00:09:46,471 --> 00:09:58,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you realize you have six consciousnesses at the same time, you can't take any of those to be one single unifying owner of all the senses.

119
00:09:59,054 --> 00:09:59,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Because which one is it?

120
00:10:00,234 --> 00:10:03,135
[SPEAKER_00]: When there are six rulers, who is in charge when they're all on the equal footing?

121
00:10:06,268 --> 00:10:19,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the plurality, recognition of that within your experience, removes the basis for gratuitous assumption of that singular sense of self.

122
00:10:22,181 --> 00:10:27,084
[SPEAKER_01]: But mind is... mind is given a sort of... Well, mind is the result of it.

123
00:10:27,725 --> 00:10:29,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is the result of the five aggregates.

124
00:10:30,127 --> 00:10:31,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is not consciousness.

125
00:10:34,166 --> 00:10:36,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness of the senses means the mind is there.

126
00:10:41,169 --> 00:10:45,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is the result of the six domains of senses.

127
00:10:45,572 --> 00:10:51,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Five domains of senses, five domains of consciousness, make up for the sixth domain, which is the mind.

128
00:10:53,396 --> 00:10:54,956
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like one, two, three, four, five, six.

129
00:10:55,076 --> 00:10:56,137
[SPEAKER_00]: It's five and one.

130
00:11:02,326 --> 00:11:09,110
[SPEAKER_00]: If you would only to have eye and eye consciousness and no other sense whatsoever, self-reflection would not be possible.

131
00:11:10,451 --> 00:11:11,392
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to think.

132
00:11:12,153 --> 00:11:16,916
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no space determined, so to speak, between these different domains.

133
00:11:18,595 --> 00:11:19,795
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to move on to, isn't there?

134
00:11:19,815 --> 00:11:20,636
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing to move on to.

135
00:11:20,656 --> 00:11:21,636
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no reference point.

136
00:11:22,056 --> 00:11:23,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you have a reference point.

137
00:11:23,737 --> 00:11:29,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You have five reference points that determine the sixth, and six in regard determines the five... the sixth, sorry.

138
00:11:30,119 --> 00:11:34,181
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind determines the five senses, but there would be no mind without the five senses defining it.

139
00:11:36,861 --> 00:11:37,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the whole point.

140
00:11:38,522 --> 00:11:40,803
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're inclined to think about consciousness or mind,

141
00:11:42,023 --> 00:11:49,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Do so, read the suttas about it, but you have to do it on the practical... you mustn't do it at the expense of your present enduring experience as a whole, right here, right now.

142
00:11:52,789 --> 00:11:56,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then it's just abstract, sort of, whatever, proliferating.

143
00:11:59,273 --> 00:12:00,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you feeling anything right now?

144
00:12:00,594 --> 00:12:01,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a feeling enduring?

145
00:12:01,675 --> 00:12:01,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

146
00:12:01,895 --> 00:12:03,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It means consciousness has to be there.

147
00:12:06,705 --> 00:12:07,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

148
00:12:07,186 --> 00:12:12,712
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to regard consciousness on the same level you regard form and forms.

149
00:12:13,252 --> 00:12:15,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Because forms are discernible only because of the senses.

150
00:12:16,536 --> 00:12:19,760
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's discernible only because there is consciousness paired with those senses.

151
00:12:26,027 --> 00:12:28,330
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I meant, like, our consciousness is out there with the things.

152
00:12:28,931 --> 00:12:35,540
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like things are conscious, but consciousness is on the same footing as things you can perceive, because that's why you're perceiving them.

153
00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:39,185
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they're present, because the domain of consciousness enables it.

154
00:12:40,813 --> 00:12:47,817
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you try to approach consciousness in positive terms, pinpoint it, you can... well, you can think you did, but that's a contradiction in terms.

155
00:12:47,837 --> 00:12:49,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, in itself, it's presence.

156
00:12:49,077 --> 00:12:51,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't explain presence of presence.

157
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:54,641
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only explain presence of something.

158
00:12:54,661 --> 00:12:58,222
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you say, well, my pure presence, there is nothing else present there.

159
00:12:58,262 --> 00:13:01,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then you take your presence to be an object that is present for you.

160
00:13:02,725 --> 00:13:04,046
[SPEAKER_00]: As an image or something.

161
00:13:04,086 --> 00:13:04,726
[SPEAKER_00]: As an image, yeah.

162
00:13:04,866 --> 00:13:07,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and exactly, the sixth sense is the mind.

163
00:13:07,208 --> 00:13:09,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind images are present, exactly, to the mind.

164
00:13:09,930 --> 00:13:13,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is mind consciousness, or consciousness in regard to the domain of mind and mind objects.

165
00:13:14,413 --> 00:13:14,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhammas.

166
00:13:29,024 --> 00:13:30,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you can also make it even more personal.

167
00:13:30,925 --> 00:13:33,407
[SPEAKER_00]: You ask yourself, okay, so why do I want to understand consciousness?

168
00:13:35,971 --> 00:13:37,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you want to get the right view.

169
00:13:37,071 --> 00:13:37,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you want the right view?

170
00:13:40,552 --> 00:13:43,873
[SPEAKER_01]: To end suffering.

171
00:13:45,173 --> 00:13:48,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, cessation of suffering is valuable to you?

172
00:13:49,214 --> 00:13:49,394
Yes.

173
00:13:50,454 --> 00:13:54,315
[SPEAKER_00]: And where's that suffering that you wanted to cease?

174
00:13:56,695 --> 00:13:57,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Right here, right now.

175
00:13:58,336 --> 00:14:00,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you suffering right here, right now?

176
00:14:03,217 --> 00:14:04,157
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd say to a degree.

177
00:14:04,997 --> 00:14:05,557
[SPEAKER_00]: To what degree?

178
00:14:06,808 --> 00:14:08,089
[SPEAKER_01]: To the degree that I'm pressured.

179
00:14:08,829 --> 00:14:10,491
[SPEAKER_01]: By what?

180
00:14:11,111 --> 00:14:15,095
[SPEAKER_01]: By the neutral feeling.

181
00:14:16,716 --> 00:14:16,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

182
00:14:16,976 --> 00:14:20,039
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not suffering, basically, on account of neutral feeling, in itself.

183
00:14:20,479 --> 00:14:26,164
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not painful, but you're suffering on account of its presence, for whatever reason, so to speak.

184
00:14:27,626 --> 00:14:30,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would you then go anywhere else?

185
00:14:31,178 --> 00:14:39,460
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to understand Consciousness, in order to get the right view, in order to be free from suffering, in order to be free from that pressure, when you have it right there with you?

186
00:14:42,941 --> 00:14:43,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I believe I can.

187
00:14:45,982 --> 00:14:46,802
[SPEAKER_00]: You believe you can what?

188
00:14:47,202 --> 00:14:52,403
[SPEAKER_01]: I can look for it anywhere else.

189
00:14:52,703 --> 00:14:53,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that a matter of belief?

190
00:14:56,124 --> 00:14:59,305
[SPEAKER_00]: You obviously can, because you are looking somewhere else.

191
00:15:01,227 --> 00:15:02,776
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm asking you, why would you do that?

192
00:15:08,027 --> 00:15:09,548
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is right here, right now.

193
00:15:10,188 --> 00:15:12,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Your liability to suffering is a problem.

194
00:15:13,169 --> 00:15:16,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Your pressure under neutral feeling is a problem.

195
00:15:16,811 --> 00:15:19,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure of the pull of the pleasant feeling is a problem.

196
00:15:19,792 --> 00:15:22,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure of the resistance to unpleasant feeling is a problem.

197
00:15:23,294 --> 00:15:25,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, not all of them are present at the same time.

198
00:15:25,335 --> 00:15:28,036
[SPEAKER_00]: One of them can only be present, which means you are liable to the others.

199
00:15:29,116 --> 00:15:31,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would you go anywhere else, if that's a problem?

200
00:15:31,677 --> 00:15:33,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you go anywhere else in order to solve that problem?

201
00:15:33,858 --> 00:15:35,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't you just understand that?

202
00:15:43,681 --> 00:15:44,121
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

203
00:15:44,201 --> 00:16:02,013
[SPEAKER_00]: More often than not, when people want to contemplate, you end up forgetting the most primordial, the most fundamental, presently enduring reasons, because of which you want to contemplate.

204
00:16:03,975 --> 00:16:08,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes people get the sense of that, but then they go too far the other way.

205
00:16:09,278 --> 00:16:10,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, don't be trying to understand.

206
00:16:10,859 --> 00:16:12,140
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing to be understood.

207
00:16:12,220 --> 00:16:13,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Just be here now.

208
00:16:15,050 --> 00:16:22,832
[SPEAKER_00]: All people know, I must understand, but then in the expense of their attempts of understanding, they abandon here and now.

209
00:16:22,872 --> 00:16:24,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, you don't want to do either.

210
00:16:26,013 --> 00:16:27,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you want to not do both.

211
00:16:28,013 --> 00:16:28,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to find the middle.

212
00:16:29,013 --> 00:16:37,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can I understand, without compromising my here and now, but without also dismissing the fact that you must understand the problem?

213
00:16:39,436 --> 00:16:41,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering is a problem, ignorance is a problem.

214
00:16:41,036 --> 00:16:43,057
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's understood, the Buddha said you'll be free from it.

215
00:16:44,255 --> 00:17:01,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't want to abandon here and now, doesn't mean you mustn't try to understand, which is when people kind of mystify their experience, by dismissing the necessity of clarity, because any notion of clarity that they have is clarity of abstract thought.

216
00:17:02,001 --> 00:17:03,022
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is that in between.

217
00:17:03,402 --> 00:17:12,208
[SPEAKER_00]: There is that concrete thinking, non-abstract thinking, that you can think and make effort to understand on account of and within the present enduring things as a whole.

218
00:17:12,248 --> 00:17:14,210
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't have to abandon your present situation.

219
00:17:14,950 --> 00:17:16,011
[SPEAKER_00]: You would not have to ignore it.

220
00:17:16,051 --> 00:17:17,592
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't abandon it, even if you want to.

221
00:17:17,632 --> 00:17:18,213
[SPEAKER_00]: You're within it.

222
00:17:18,433 --> 00:17:19,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can ignore it.

223
00:17:23,336 --> 00:17:27,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have an emotional consciousness, I feel inspired to understand it, absolutely great.

224
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:31,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't allow that to take you away from present enduring experience as a whole.

225
00:17:32,045 --> 00:17:40,071
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, at the same time, don't allow present enduring experience as a whole to make you dull and lazy and kind of complacent.

226
00:17:40,091 --> 00:17:41,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't make the effort to understand.

227
00:17:43,854 --> 00:17:48,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Present enduring experience as a whole, that's what needs to be understood while being present.

228
00:17:49,438 --> 00:17:50,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I always come back to.

229
00:17:51,540 --> 00:17:52,000
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my...

230
00:17:53,534 --> 00:17:54,615
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a current enduring feeling.

231
00:17:55,035 --> 00:17:56,337
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I feel overall?

232
00:17:56,397 --> 00:17:56,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Neutral.

233
00:17:57,137 --> 00:18:00,060
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's painful, because I'm kind of confused in regard to it.

234
00:18:00,781 --> 00:18:01,822
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to be free from it.

235
00:18:02,983 --> 00:18:10,350
[SPEAKER_00]: That freedom is found right there and then, within that relationship of what you feel and pain you're liable to, because of that pressure.

236
00:18:11,411 --> 00:18:14,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And that relationship is the relationship of craving.

237
00:18:14,594 --> 00:18:16,796
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there is only one root of suffering.

238
00:18:17,336 --> 00:18:17,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving.

239
00:18:18,457 --> 00:18:18,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

240
00:18:21,651 --> 00:18:24,252
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how diverse your suffering can be.

241
00:18:24,372 --> 00:18:32,853
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is suffering present in your experience of any kind, of any extent, on any level, it's because craving is present.

242
00:18:32,913 --> 00:18:35,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving in regard to what you're currently feeling.

243
00:18:39,035 --> 00:18:49,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the craving is not maintained anymore by you, then feeling will just be the feeling, perception will just be perception.

244
00:18:50,966 --> 00:18:54,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas the Buddha said, in the seen, there'll just be the seen, in the heard, just be the heard.

245
00:18:54,407 --> 00:18:59,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you ignore everything else that's in it, but because you stop misconceiving anything else that's not in it.

246
00:19:02,550 --> 00:19:06,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter the extent of the sights, the extent of the sounds, smell, taste, touch, thoughts.

247
00:19:09,032 --> 00:19:13,173
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not misconceive it through your craving, through looking elsewhere.

248
00:19:18,242 --> 00:19:19,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can still think.

249
00:19:19,182 --> 00:19:20,323
[SPEAKER_00]: You can still think abstractly.

250
00:19:20,503 --> 00:19:22,123
[SPEAKER_00]: You can think about past, about the future.

251
00:19:22,163 --> 00:19:28,264
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that would be presently misconceived through resisting how you feel in regard to it.

252
00:19:29,525 --> 00:19:30,385
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was always a problem.

253
00:19:31,385 --> 00:19:34,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing where the problem is.

254
00:19:34,626 --> 00:19:37,567
[SPEAKER_00]: So, trying to solve it, resolve it elsewhere.

255
00:19:39,467 --> 00:19:42,828
[SPEAKER_00]: And the problem is, in regard to present enduring feeling that you have right here, right now,

256
00:19:44,239 --> 00:19:54,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And your attitude of resisting it, either towards, away, or everywhere and nowhere, ignoring it.

257
00:20:05,934 --> 00:20:07,295
[SPEAKER_00]: You find the pain of the pressure.

258
00:20:10,271 --> 00:20:14,942
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, stop resisting the pain of it and let the pressure be.

259
00:20:50,728 --> 00:21:07,039
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only through the craving that you can move, sort of like, look for a direction where the... Yeah, there has to be a first motivator, so to speak.

260
00:21:08,362 --> 00:21:11,163
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha meant by craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving.

261
00:21:11,723 --> 00:21:17,024
[SPEAKER_00]: You crave to be free from suffering in the same way that your craving makes you suffer in the first place.

262
00:21:17,904 --> 00:21:22,525
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you then discern where the actual problem of that is, you can learn how to stop craving.

263
00:21:23,546 --> 00:21:24,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop fueling the craving.

264
00:21:25,806 --> 00:21:31,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving is not like a choice you make, but it's a result of the choices you make through craving.

265
00:21:32,148 --> 00:21:33,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you end up maintaining the craving.

266
00:21:35,433 --> 00:21:37,836
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you have craving to understand freedom from suffering.

267
00:21:37,876 --> 00:21:44,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, out of that craving, you make the effort, you do the suttas, you practice sense restraint, you celibate, you keep the precepts.

268
00:21:45,445 --> 00:21:46,546
[SPEAKER_00]: All that is rooted in craving.

269
00:21:46,626 --> 00:21:46,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Fine.

270
00:21:46,906 --> 00:21:50,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's no problem with that.

271
00:21:52,753 --> 00:21:56,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that's all you do, means you're never going to look at the domain where the actual craving is.

272
00:21:57,803 --> 00:22:12,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But, say, once you start doing that, and then you recognize, okay, now I need to see where the craving is within this experience that's now restrained, within the precepts and so on, then you get to understand that even your effort to understand consciousness, for example, oh, is rooted in craving.

273
00:22:14,551 --> 00:22:16,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that doesn't mean I should not understand consciousness.

274
00:22:16,732 --> 00:22:18,753
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means I should not do it out of craving.

275
00:22:20,934 --> 00:22:22,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how you not do it out of craving?

276
00:22:22,335 --> 00:22:25,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by first of all not allowing that craving to take you elsewhere,

277
00:22:27,402 --> 00:22:33,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Other than where that consciousness or whatever aspect of dhamma can be understood, which is presently enduring experience as a whole.

278
00:22:36,187 --> 00:22:41,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's sometimes hard to see that, oh wait, because it's, you know, it's good to make effort to understand the dhamma.

279
00:22:41,169 --> 00:22:41,449
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

280
00:22:43,350 --> 00:22:52,754
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to basically sharpen and make that effort more accurate, because otherwise you'll end up out of craving to understand the dhamma.

281
00:22:53,014 --> 00:22:56,256
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up maintaining your craving because of which you don't understand the dhamma.

282
00:23:00,783 --> 00:23:05,164
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, I want to understand consciousness, or I want to understand feeling, or I even want to understand craving.

283
00:23:06,105 --> 00:23:15,088
[SPEAKER_00]: First thing is not to allow that inspiration to do so, to take me anywhere else and think on any other terms, except present enduring experience as a whole.

284
00:23:15,588 --> 00:23:16,368
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I feel right now?

285
00:23:16,868 --> 00:23:18,749
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my present enduring posture right now?

286
00:23:20,069 --> 00:23:20,569
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I like?

287
00:23:20,589 --> 00:23:28,792
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, you look basically and ask yourself, oh, are there things in me, right here, right now,

288
00:23:33,170 --> 00:23:38,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of which, by not abandoning them, I can experience lust in the future.

289
00:23:40,119 --> 00:23:45,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Are there things right here, right now, in me, because of which, by not abandoning them, I would experience ill-will?

290
00:23:46,288 --> 00:23:49,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I would get hindrances, or anything... Oh, are there?

291
00:23:49,309 --> 00:23:49,689
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

292
00:23:49,769 --> 00:23:51,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because of that, there are.

293
00:23:51,749 --> 00:23:58,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I need to know how can I not be liable to these unwholesome things because of which craving gets perpetuated.

294
00:23:59,891 --> 00:24:08,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on account of your craving, you descend enough to stop yourself from going elsewhere, looking elsewhere, because of that craving, and you start looking at the present enduring situation.

295
00:24:09,473 --> 00:24:11,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind free from greed, aversion, delusion right now?

296
00:24:12,634 --> 00:24:13,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind gripped with the desire?

297
00:24:13,934 --> 00:24:13,994
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

298
00:24:14,935 --> 00:24:16,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind gripped with irritation and ill will?

299
00:24:17,379 --> 00:24:17,539
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

300
00:24:18,180 --> 00:24:19,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind gripped with discontent?

301
00:24:21,064 --> 00:24:21,265
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

302
00:24:22,066 --> 00:24:22,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

303
00:24:22,927 --> 00:24:24,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, next set of questions.

304
00:24:24,750 --> 00:24:28,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Can my mind remain non-infected with desire?

305
00:24:30,137 --> 00:24:32,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I know how to maintain it, so the desire cannot enter it?

306
00:24:33,620 --> 00:24:36,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Can my mind remain above ill-will, so ill-will cannot enter it?

307
00:24:36,923 --> 00:24:40,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Can my mind remain above discontent, so discontent cannot enter it?

308
00:24:41,247 --> 00:24:46,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way to figure it out, to... the only way to accomplish that, is to understand how that is done.

309
00:24:47,747 --> 00:24:49,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you're just wishful thinking.

310
00:24:49,248 --> 00:24:55,313
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just hoping that, okay, now I've done something that resulted in me being currently free from desire, but I have no idea really directly.

311
00:24:55,373 --> 00:25:00,057
[SPEAKER_00]: I have no direct insight knowing it's because of this or because of that, because I abandoned this and cultivated that.

312
00:25:00,797 --> 00:25:03,540
[SPEAKER_00]: And because you don't know that, that's why desire will come back.

313
00:25:03,560 --> 00:25:04,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Your will will come back.

314
00:25:05,621 --> 00:25:06,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you're not free.

315
00:25:26,828 --> 00:25:30,610
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I contemplate that without losing the current base?

316
00:25:30,830 --> 00:25:32,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are you feeling right now?

317
00:25:32,951 --> 00:25:33,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral.

318
00:25:33,732 --> 00:25:34,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Neutral.

319
00:25:39,435 --> 00:25:41,036
[SPEAKER_00]: How about now?

320
00:25:41,076 --> 00:25:41,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

321
00:25:42,536 --> 00:25:45,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's happening to that feeling all this time?

322
00:25:45,718 --> 00:25:46,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Enduring.

323
00:25:46,799 --> 00:25:48,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have a say in that endurance?

324
00:25:49,160 --> 00:25:49,400
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

325
00:25:50,941 --> 00:25:53,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you... did you have a say in this creation?

326
00:25:54,495 --> 00:25:55,435
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know where it came from.

327
00:25:56,156 --> 00:25:56,956
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

328
00:25:57,576 --> 00:25:58,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you tell it to go away?

329
00:26:00,377 --> 00:26:01,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that I can think of.

330
00:26:02,858 --> 00:26:06,200
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you then try to relate to it?

331
00:26:08,801 --> 00:26:09,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Or have a relationship with it?

332
00:26:10,362 --> 00:26:11,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Or act out of it?

333
00:26:12,202 --> 00:26:14,203
[SPEAKER_00]: That feeling doesn't even know about you.

334
00:26:14,223 --> 00:26:15,023
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for it.

335
00:26:15,944 --> 00:26:21,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Clearly, it has its own arising, has its own ceasing, has its own domain.

336
00:26:22,587 --> 00:26:23,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the problem?

337
00:26:28,818 --> 00:26:29,499
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you do.

338
00:26:30,599 --> 00:26:31,000
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize.

339
00:26:31,020 --> 00:26:32,521
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that you forget that.

340
00:26:33,982 --> 00:26:36,043
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you forget that, you act out of the feeling.

341
00:26:37,524 --> 00:26:39,025
[SPEAKER_00]: And you forget that you have no say.

342
00:26:39,045 --> 00:26:41,547
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot penetrate or enter the feeling or interfere with it.

343
00:26:42,728 --> 00:26:47,331
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you act towards sensuality, act out of ill-will, act out of boredom.

344
00:26:48,632 --> 00:26:52,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, cultivate and re-keep, re-interrogate yourself.

345
00:26:54,418 --> 00:27:03,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it becomes mechanical, sit down, tighten down the effort a bit more, and then find the new sets of questions, they will bring you back to the same thing.

346
00:27:04,626 --> 00:27:06,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is what's my current body posture.

347
00:27:07,188 --> 00:27:07,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you ever say in it?

348
00:27:07,948 --> 00:27:09,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you ever say in the beating heart?

349
00:27:09,930 --> 00:27:11,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you ever say in things that occur, no?

350
00:27:12,512 --> 00:27:17,136
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only sort of squirm and resist when they're there.

351
00:27:18,316 --> 00:27:20,178
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, you realize, well, that makes no difference.

352
00:27:20,198 --> 00:27:22,620
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a result of me already being affected by those things.

353
00:27:25,049 --> 00:27:29,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, maintain the context that whatever's presently enduring, don't lose the sight of it.

354
00:27:29,331 --> 00:27:33,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, whatever's presently enduring in terms of what you're feeling, don't lose the sight of it.

355
00:27:33,954 --> 00:27:36,636
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will not be able to act too far out of the craving.

356
00:27:39,777 --> 00:27:44,520
[SPEAKER_00]: And the less far you act out, the clearer the whole nature of acting out becomes.

357
00:27:45,701 --> 00:27:46,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop feeding it, eh?

358
00:27:47,121 --> 00:27:48,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much, yeah.

359
00:27:48,442 --> 00:27:53,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop feeding it until it loses its strength on account of you not providing its food.

360
00:27:53,787 --> 00:27:58,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can overpower it, and subdue it, and tame it.

361
00:28:06,852 --> 00:28:07,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, you want to train a dog.

362
00:28:08,773 --> 00:28:11,474
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you make it... you do it with food, basically.

363
00:28:12,594 --> 00:28:15,055
[SPEAKER_00]: When he's hungry, the dog's just eating a lot.

364
00:28:15,956 --> 00:28:16,996
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't... he won't listen to you.

365
00:28:17,449 --> 00:28:18,569
[SPEAKER_00]: If he hasn't listened to you before.

366
00:28:19,830 --> 00:28:21,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to withdraw it from its feeding ground.

367
00:28:22,410 --> 00:28:28,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it perceives that it can always eat whenever it wants to eat, however it wants to eat, it doesn't need you.

368
00:28:28,712 --> 00:28:29,412
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just going to do it.

369
00:28:30,813 --> 00:28:33,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you restrain it from going to eat whenever it wants to eat.

370
00:28:33,834 --> 00:28:36,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it realizes that the only way it can get food is through you.

371
00:28:36,954 --> 00:28:39,035
[SPEAKER_00]: In which case, it better starts doing what you want it to do.

372
00:28:39,942 --> 00:28:48,005
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I'm using the simile of the dogs all the time, but it's the same in the suttas, just in regard to other animals, but it's the same principle.

373
00:28:49,625 --> 00:28:53,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the five senses are the five wild animals with their respective feeding grounds.

374
00:28:54,787 --> 00:28:58,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Monkey, crocodile, boar, I can't remember the other ones.

375
00:28:58,828 --> 00:28:59,328
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

376
00:28:59,348 --> 00:29:04,950
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, in order for you to train them, in order for you to make them obey you,

377
00:29:06,232 --> 00:29:08,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intentions, your mind, not other way around.

378
00:29:08,894 --> 00:29:12,177
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to restrict the extent of free roaming.

379
00:29:16,220 --> 00:29:19,762
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, they'll realize the only way they can get these things is if they listen to you.

380
00:29:19,962 --> 00:29:21,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they'll sit down and wait for food.

381
00:29:25,126 --> 00:29:26,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the problem, by the way.

382
00:29:26,267 --> 00:29:27,588
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that they go hungry or something.

383
00:29:27,608 --> 00:29:30,230
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that they eat carelessly everything.

384
00:29:31,211 --> 00:29:32,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Even things they shouldn't be eating.

385
00:29:42,098 --> 00:29:42,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what I mean.

386
00:29:42,558 --> 00:29:56,007
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep a corner of your eye at the back of your mind always, in that peripheral context of what is the currently enduring feeling, what are my currently enduring intentions, what do I want to do, what for, to what extent.

387
00:29:57,388 --> 00:30:00,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not... it's not something I must keep this in front of my mind all the time.

388
00:30:00,390 --> 00:30:00,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

389
00:30:00,930 --> 00:30:05,033
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start doing it rightly, you have more and more space to relax within it.

390
00:30:06,894 --> 00:30:09,916
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to first subdue the animals.

391
00:30:12,517 --> 00:30:13,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Turn them down.

392
00:30:19,712 --> 00:30:21,075
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, do the discomfort of it.

